<!--
    Type-recovery campaign, coreutils `fmt` -O2 (stripped): `main` @0x26a0 calls
    one static function three times and kuna renders three different arities,
    the longest reading a local nothing assigns.

        unsigned long v9;  // rdx        <- never assigned
        unsigned long v11; // rdx        <- never assigned
          v10 = v9;  ...  v10 = v11;
          v12 = sub_3700(stdin,"-",v10);   // three arguments
          v3  = sub_3700(stdin,v6);        // two
          v12 &= sub_3700(v5);             // one

    DWARF says `fmt(FILE *f, char const *name)`: two parameters.

    Root cause (P4, active-input trial scoring).  `Heritage::guardCalls`
    (heritage.cc:1444) plants an INDIRECT *creation* for a killed-by-call
    register that is also a possible OUTPUT location of the callee's model.
    rdx is one on x86-64, because a 16-byte value returns in rax:rdx, and
    `AncestorRealistic::enterNode` (funcdata_varnode.cc:2043) answers
    pop_success for exactly that op, recording `ParamTrial::setIndCreateFormed`.
    With `onlyOpUse` satisfied the trial is active and
    `ParamListStandard::fillinMap` marks every active trial used, so a register
    the caller never wrote for this call becomes its trailing argument.
    Upstream reads that same bit back on the RETURN side only
    (`ParamListStandardOut::fillinMap`, fspec.cc:1721), which is why stock
    Ghidra emits the identical three-argument call and names the phantom
    `extraout_RDX`.

    THIS FIXTURE IS THE NEGATIVE CONTROL for the clause that decides the drop.
    `option argclobber on` drops the trailing argument only when the CALLEE's
    own recovered prototype says the register is free, and that prototype
    reaches a call site only through `protoorder`, which parks it when a
    whole-binary `decompile-all` decompiles the callee first.  This harness
    decompiles ONE function from a byte image: no callee was decompiled first,
    nothing is parked, `target` has stated nothing about itself, and a rule that
    deletes an argument may not read silence as permission.  So the caller's
    side here is the exact shape the option exists for: rdx is a two-input
    join and NEITHER input is a value the caller put there. On one path
    `clobber` destroyed the register, on the other an `idivl` left its remainder
    in edx while the caller went on to use only the quotient. The argument
    still survives both passes.

    The POSITIVE witness, the same construct as a real program so that
    `decompile-all` decompiles `target` first, is
    tests/cli/argclobber-drops-a-trailing-clobbered-argument.json over
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/argclobber_x86_64, where the
    call renders `sub_401031(a0,5)` and both the phantom `// rdx` local and the
    dead remainder are gone.  tests/cli/argclobber-off-keeps-the-clobbered-
    argument.json is its option-off arm.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring".  The P0 assertion
    `option argclobber on|off` decides whether the trailing argument of a call
    may be dropped when nothing on the caller's side put a value in its
    register.  Pass 1 pins the shipped `off`; pass 2 pins that `on` alone is not
    enough.
-->
